# Git — Rewrite History Safely

Created: 2026-02-02 05:37 UTC  
Last reviewed: 2026-02-02 05:37 UTC

History rewriting is sometimes useful (fixup commits, clean PRs), but easy to do dangerously.

## Squash / Reword Recent Commits (Interactive Rebase)
```sh
git fetch origin
git rebase -i origin/main
```

Common actions:
- `pick` → keep commit
- `reword` → change message
- `squash` / `fixup` → merge into previous commit

## Fix the Last Commit Message
```sh
git commit --amend
```

## Force Push (Safer Variant)
Only when you rewrote history and the remote must match:
```sh
git push --force-with-lease
```

Why `--force-with-lease`:
- prevents clobbering remote updates you don’t have locally

## “I Rebasing Broke Everything” Recovery
- If you haven’t pushed: use `git reflog` to find a safe point and reset.
```sh
git reflog
git reset --hard <sha>
```

- If you did push: coordinate with the team before resetting shared history.

